{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we start by understanding the conditions given. We need to find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{n_i}{k}\\) for \\(1 \\leq i \\leq 70\\), where \\(k = \\lfloor \\sqrt[3]{n_i} \\rfloor\\) and \\(k\\) divides \\(n_i\\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\(n_i\\) in terms of \\(k\\). Since \\(k = \\lfloor \\sqrt[3]{n_i} \\rfloor\\), we know that:\n\\[ k \\leq \\sqrt[3]{n_i} < k+1 \\]\nCubing all parts of the inequality, we get:\n\\[ k^3 \\leq n_i < (k+1)^3 \\]\nSince \\(k\\) divides \\(n_i\\), we can write \\(n_i = k^3 + m \\cdot k\\) where \\(0 \\leq m \\cdot k < (k+1)^3 - k^3\\). Simplifying the upper bound, we get:\n\\[ (k+1)^3 - k^3 = k^3 + 3k^2 + 3k + 1 - k^3 = 3k^2 + 3k + 1 \\]\nThus, \\(0 \\leq m \\cdot k < 3k^2 + 3k + 1\\). Since \\(k\\) divides \\(n_i\\), \\(m\\) must be an integer such that \\(0 \\leq m < \\frac{3k^2 + 3k + 1}{k} = 3k + 3 + \\frac{1}{k}\\). Therefore, \\(m\\) can take any integer value from 0 to \\(3k + 2\\), giving us \\(3k + 3\\) possible values for \\(m\\).\n\n","prefix_end":935,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1191,553,8660,279,4682,2661,13,1205,1184,311,1477,279,7192,897,315,1124,11520,37018,91362,5318,15170,74,11035,8,369,17767,16,1124,273,80,600,1124,273,80,220,22,15,59,701,1380,17767,74,284,1124,11008,3993,1124,26888,58,18,15370,77,5318,92,1124,8052,3993,57758,323,17767,74,57758,64828,17767,77,5318,59,3593,5338,11,1077,594,3158,17767,77,5318,57758,304,3793,315,17767,74,59,568,8704,17767,74,284,1124,11008,3993,1124,26888,58,18,15370,77,5318,92,1124,8052,3993,59,701,582,1414,429,510,78045,595,1124,273,80,1124,26888,58,18,15370,77,5318,92,366,595,10,16,1124,921,34,392,287,678,5479,315,279,31205,11,582,633,510,78045,595,61,18,1124,273,80,308,5318,366,320,74,10,16,29776,18,1124,921,12549,17767,74,57758,64828,17767,77,5318,59,701,582,646,3270,17767